WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.560 --> 00:00:10.240
Commencement is the signature event on the&nbsp;
MIT campus. So then to be asked to create the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.240 --> 00:00:16.320
opening music video to this big event, also&nbsp;
knowing that tens of thousands of people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:23.440
were gonna be ready and waiting to watch this&nbsp;
live. That was a real honor and a real privilege

00:00:23.440 --> 00:00:29.120
[Instrumental music playing]

00:00:29.120 --> 00:00:32.800
There's a big dream with this piece. I remember&nbsp;
when Fred first told us about this piece,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.800 --> 00:00:37.120
he was like everyone's going to be in it.&nbsp;
Every single person we could possibly get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.120 --> 00:00:39.840
is going to be in this piece,&nbsp;
and Fred a man of big ideas.&nbsp;

00:00:40.560 --> 00:00:45.600
We're talking about marshaling&nbsp;
200 people, it was a little crazy.

00:00:45.702 --> 00:00:48.846
[Instrumental music playing]

00:00:49.040 --> 00:00:51.760
It wasn't just going to be Jazz Band. It&nbsp;
was going to be the Vocal Jazz Ensemble.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.760 --> 00:00:53.040
It was going to be a cappella groups. It was going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.040 --> 00:00:57.137
to be MITSO. It was going to be Wind&nbsp;
Ensemble. It was going to be Choir.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.137 --> 00:01:02.121
[Instrumental music playing]

00:01:02.240 --> 00:01:07.137
My first thought was wow this is going to be&nbsp;
such an epic way to celebrate commencement.

00:01:07.137 --> 00:01:15.040
♪ To the list of things that make me sad ♪&nbsp;

00:01:15.040 --> 00:01:18.560
The lyrics from the piece were taken&nbsp;
from poetry submitted by MIT students.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:27.760
The tone of it was really personal. A lot of it&nbsp;
kind of achingly painful and difficult but with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.760 --> 00:01:35.191
glimmers of hope and sense of trying to prevail,&nbsp;
trying to overcome the challenges of the pandemic.

00:01:35.191 --> 00:01:41.210
♪ Just gray and gray and gray ♪

00:01:41.210 --> 00:01:45.862
[Instrumental music playing]

00:01:46.080 --> 00:01:50.453
I felt very much that that was reflective&nbsp;
of our pandemic experience. &nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.453 --> 00:01:55.920
It started out really tough and by the time that we&nbsp;were recording this we were flourishing.

00:01:55.920 --> 00:02:04.160
♪ Look at the sky and remember ♪

00:02:04.160 --> 00:02:08.160
We're going to acknowledge the&nbsp;
seriousness and the devastation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.160 --> 00:02:13.520
that the pandemic has caused. At the same time&nbsp;
we're going to try to learn from this experience&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.520 --> 00:02:18.587
and not just go back to normal because&nbsp;
there is no more going back to normal.

00:02:18.587 --> 00:02:22.640
♪ Look at the sky ♪

00:02:22.640 --> 00:02:27.846
Being outside in the Stata amphitheater and&nbsp;
playing trumpet alongside the Festival Jazz&nbsp;&nbsp;Ensemble. 

00:02:27.846 --> 00:02:32.720
It was an awesome experience because&nbsp;
I actually hadn't really played with people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.720 --> 00:02:39.120
like live for an entire year, basically.&nbsp;
There was a certain energy that everyone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.120 --> 00:02:44.960
had because we finally got to see everybody&nbsp;
together in the same place playing together.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.960 --> 00:02:49.840
It felt like a great send-off&nbsp;
sort of to our senior year.

00:02:49.840 --> 00:02:54.880
♪ Overwriting the written constellations ♪

00:02:54.880 --> 00:03:00.596
It was pretty amazing that they moved a piano&nbsp;
and drum kit outside the Stata.

00:03:00.596 --> 00:03:05.847
 A beautiful&nbsp;&nbsp;upright piano being able to play on the&nbsp;
real instrument made such a difference.

00:03:05.920 --> 00:03:10.880
♪ I trace my finger ♪

00:03:10.880 --> 00:03:18.160
I want the music video to bring something to the&nbsp;
music that isn't there by just hearing the song.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.160 --> 00:03:23.933
[Instrumental music playing]

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:27.925
If you're able to cut to a certain&nbsp;
instrumentalist at a certain time,

00:03:27.925 --> 00:03:33.600
you're&nbsp;drawing the listener to hearing such a booming&nbsp;
instrument that comes in right at that time.

00:03:33.600 --> 00:03:37.342
♪ crack in the door ♪

00:03:37.342 --> 00:03:41.373
[Instrumental music playing]

00:03:41.440 --> 00:03:44.720
What i wanted to do in the shooting is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.720 --> 00:03:49.360
to have an improvisational aspect to it&nbsp;
so that all the camera people present&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.000 --> 00:03:57.232
could really use their eyes and ears to frame what&nbsp;
looked like the right thing to see at that time.

00:03:57.232 --> 00:04:06.000
[Instrumental music playing]

00:04:06.080 --> 00:04:08.320
Jamshied, I think is really good when it comes to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.320 --> 00:04:11.280
writing for guitar he really gets&nbsp;
what makes guitar song good. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.280 --> 00:04:15.120
so he has his own guitar and he sent the video&nbsp;
of him playing the part. 

00:04:15.120 --> 00:04:18.722
Hey Sebastian there's&nbsp;&nbsp;probably other ways to play the chords but&nbsp;

00:04:18.722 --> 00:04:23.019
this sounded good to me on a DADGAD tuning.

00:04:23.019 --> 00:04:30.193
[Guitar music playing]

00:04:30.193 --> 00:04:33.200
So you'll make this thing right? Okay thanks man.

00:04:33.200 --> 00:04:35.707
[Instrumental music playing]

00:04:35.707 --> 00:04:38.400
♪ This is the beginning ♪

00:04:38.400 --> 00:04:41.440
It's using what we call an open tuning.
So we detune the strings of the guitar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.440 --> 00:04:45.440
to be able to play different chords and the&nbsp;
whole guitar sounds different when you do that.

00:04:45.440 --> 00:04:48.880
♪ Again and again
It is the beginning ♪

00:04:48.880 --> 00:04:52.320
A good guitar part can be really fun to&nbsp;
play and this is definitely one of those.

00:04:52.320 --> 00:04:54.534
Such a climactic moment in the music. 

00:04:54.534 --> 00:04:57.232
That is what you play music for moments like those.

00:04:58.170 --> 00:05:10.053
[Instrumental music playing]

00:05:10.053 --> 00:05:15.467
♪ We are fragile we are precious ♪

00:05:15.467 --> 00:05:18.720
♪ We are fragile ♪

00:05:18.720 --> 00:05:24.160
The music department has given so&nbsp;
much to me in terms of experiences&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.160 --> 00:05:31.818
and friendships. So it was really great to be able&nbsp;
to represent music at MIT through this performance.

00:05:32.360 --> 00:05:39.253
♪ We are fragile
 We are precious ♪

00:05:41.085 --> 00:05:51.107
♪ Who will listen ♪

